    <body>
      <div type="clause" n="72">
        <head><ref type="lawCode" target="law:Ine">Ine</ref> 72</head>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="ang" source="#liebermann" n="72" corresp="#wit-e"><pb n="120" ed="#liebermann"/>CXVI. Gif mon wergildðeof<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="10" target="#Ine_72.lnote.oe.10"/> gefehð, ⁊ he losige ðy dæge þa<hi rend="italic">m<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="11" target="#Ine_72.lnote.oe.11"/></hi> monnum ðe hine<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="12" target="#Ine_72.lnote.oe.12"/> gefoð, þeah hine mon<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="13" target="#Ine_72.lnote.oe.13"/> gefó ymb niht, nah him mon mare æt ðon<hi rend="italic">ne</hi> fulwite.</ab>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="ang" source="#liebermann" n="72" corresp="#wit-ot-frags"><pb n="120" ed="#liebermann"/>...þeof gefehþ, ... monnum þe hine ...</ab>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="ang" source="#liebermann" n="72" corresp="#wit-h"><pb n="120" ed="#liebermann"/>CXVI. Gif mon wergildþeof gefehð, ⁊ he losie<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="7" target="#Ine_72.lnote.oe.7-2"/> ðy dæge þam monnum þe hine gefoð, þeah hine mon<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="4" target="#Ine_72.lnote.oe.4-2"/> gefó ymb nyht<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="3" target="#Ine_72.lnote.oe.3"/>, nah hi<hi rend="italic">m</hi> man mare æt ðonne fulwite.</ab>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="ang" source="#liebermann" n="72" corresp="#wit-b-no-prol"><pb n="121" ed="#liebermann"/>Be wergildðeofes forefenge. Gyf mon wergyldðeof gefehð, ⁊ he losie ðu<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="1" target="#Ine_72.lnote.oe.1"/> dæge ðam mannu<hi rend="italic">m</hi> ðe hine gefoð, ðeah hine man gefó ymbe niht, nah him mon mare æt ðonon fulwíte.</ab>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="la" source="#liebermann" n="72" corresp="#wit-q"><pb n="121" ed="#liebermann"/>Si weregildo<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="4" target="#Ine_72.lnote.oe.4"/>-fur<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="5" target="#Ine_72.lnote.oe.5"/> capiatur, et ipsa die perdant eum qui ceperant<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="6" target="#Ine_72.lnote.oe.6"/> illum, si iterum<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="7" target="#Ine_72.lnote.oe.7"/> capiatur ipsa nocte, non habebitur amplius inde quam plena wita.</ab>
        <ab type="editionText" xml:lang="ang" source="#attenborough" corresp="#wit-e"><pb n="58" ed="#attenborough"/>[Be wergildðeofes forefenge.] Gif mon wergildðeof gefehð, ⁊ he losige ðy dæge þam monnum ðe hine gefoð, þeah hine mon gefó ymb niht, nah him mon mare æt ðonne<ptr type="editorNote" n="15" target="#Ine_72.attnote.fn.15"/> fulwite.</ab>
        <ab type="editionText" xml:lang="ang" source="#jurasinski-and-oliver" n="74" corresp="#wit-e"><pb n="432" ed="#jurasinski-and-oliver"/>{CXVI} Gif mon wergildðeof gefehð, ⁊ he losige ðy dæge þa<hi rend="italic">m</hi> monnum ðe hine gefoð, þeah hine mon gefo ymb niht, nah him mon mare æt ðon<hi rend="italic">ne</hi> fulwite.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="de" source="#liebermann"><pb n="121" ed="#liebermann"/>Wenn ein Verbrecher<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="1" target="#Ine_72.lnote.de.1"/> [bei<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="2" target="#Ine_72.lnote.de.2"/> einer in] Wergeld [verfällenden That] gefasst wird, er jedoch<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="3" target="#Ine_72.lnote.de.3"/> an demselben Tage den Leuten, die ihn fangen, entflieht, so erhält man<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="4" target="#Ine_72.lnote.de.4"/> von ihm nicht mehr als volles Strafgeld<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="5" target="#Ine_72.lnote.de.5"/>, selbst<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="6" target="#Ine_72.lnote.de.6"/> falls er [noch] zur Nacht [wieder] eingefangen wird.<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="7" target="#Ine_72.lnote.de.7"/></ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude" ana="#fromGerman">If an offender [in a deed incurring] wergeld is caught, but escapes on the same day from the people who catch him, then no more than the full fine is to be had from him, even if he is caught [again] by nightfall.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#attenborough" corresp="#wit-e"><pb n="59" ed="#attenborough"/>If a thief, who has forfeited his wergeld<ptr type="editorNote" n="1" target="#Ine_72.attnote.n.1"/>, is caught, and if he escapes the same day from his captors, yet is [re]captured before the night is passed, no more than the full fine<ptr type="editorNote" n="2" target="#Ine_72.attnote.n.2"/> shall be exacted from them<ptr type="editorNote" n="3" target="#Ine_72.attnote.n.3"/>.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#whitelock"><pb n="407" ed="#whitelock"/>If a thief who would be liable to pay his wergild<ptr type="editorNote" n="5" target="#Ine_72.whinote.n.5"/> is captured, and he escapes on the same day from the men who have caught him, yet he is recaptured the same night, one has no right to more than full fine from them.<ptr type="editorNote" n="1" target="#Ine_72.whinote.n.1"/></ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#jurasinski-and-oliver" corresp="#wit-e"><pb n="433" ed="#jurasinski-and-oliver"/>If a thief is caught who owes wergild for his offense, and he escapes on the day that he was caught from the men who caught him, but he is caught again before night, [the captors] need pay no more than the full fine.</ab>
        <note type="comparison" source="#jurasinski-and-oliver" n="432"><label>compare</label> <ref target="clause:Ine_28">Ine 28</ref> <quote>Compare to §28.</quote></note>
        <note type="comparison" source="#jurasinski-and-oliver" n="433"><label>compare</label> <ref target="clause:Ine_28">Ine 28</ref> <quote>Compare to §28 and §37. According to §28, a person who captures a thief is due a reward [...] according to §37, those who let him go are subject to a fine.</quote></note>
        <note type="comparison" source="#jurasinski-and-oliver" n="433"><label>compare</label> <ref target="clause:Ine_36">Ine 36</ref> <quote>Compare to §28 and §37. According to §28, a person who captures a thief is due a reward [...] according to §37, those who let him go are subject to a fine.</quote></note>
        <note type="comparedBy" source="#jurasinski-and-oliver" n="395"><ref target="clause:Ine_28-1">Ine 28.1</ref></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Ine_72.lnote.oe.1" n="1" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">121</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">geänd. in</hi> ði</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">altered to ði</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Ine_72.lnote.oe.4" n="4" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">121</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">so KCo;</hi> -ld <hi rend="italic">Or;</hi> -ldus <hi rend="italic">übrige</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">thus KCo; -ld Or; -ldus the others</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Ine_72.lnote.oe.5" n="5" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">121</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">ca. fur <hi rend="italic">Lond</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">ca. fur Lond</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Ine_72.lnote.oe.6" n="6" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">121</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">eum ceperunt <hi rend="italic">statt</hi> ce. il. <hi rend="italic">Lond</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">eum ceperunt instead of ce. il. Lond</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Ine_72.lnote.oe.7" n="7" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">121</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">so HkBr;</hi> iter <hi rend="italic">M;</hi> item <hi rend="italic">Lond R T</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">thus HkBr; iter M; item Lond R T</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Ine_72.lnote.oe.7-2" n="7" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">120</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">vor</hi> e <hi rend="italic">ein Buchst. radirt</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">a letter erased before e</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Ine_72.lnote.oe.4-2" n="4" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">120</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">o <hi rend="italic">geänd. in</hi> a</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">o altered to a</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Ine_72.lnote.oe.3" n="3" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">120</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">y <hi rend="italic">auf Rasur</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">y on an erasure</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Ine_72.lnote.oe.10" n="10" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">120</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">-gyldþ. <hi rend="italic">So Ld</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">-gyldþ. So Ld</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Ine_72.lnote.oe.11" n="11" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">120</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">ðæm man. <hi rend="italic">Ld</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">ðæm man. Ld</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Ine_72.lnote.oe.12" n="12" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">120</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">hin <hi rend="italic">Ld</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">hin Ld</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Ine_72.lnote.oe.13" n="13" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">120</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">man <hi rend="italic">Ld</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">man Ld</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="Ine_72.lnote.de.1" n="1" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">81</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">1</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">Nicht bloss ein Dieb; <hi rend="italic">vgl. Gl</hi> Wergeld 26. 30</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">Not merely a thief; <hi rend="italic">cf. Gl</hi> Wergeld 26. 30</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="Ine_72.lnote.de.2" n="2" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">81</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">1</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">Vgl. Gl</hi> handhaft 10</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude"><hi rend="italic">Cf. Gl</hi> handhaft 10</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="Ine_72.lnote.de.3" n="3" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">81</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">1</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">S. o.</hi> 71³</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude"><hi rend="italic">See above</hi> 71³</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="Ine_72.lnote.de.4" n="4" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">81</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">1</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">Der Gerichtsherr, nicht ‘Einfänger’ (wie Price meint), der ja nie <hi rend="spaced">Strafgeld</hi> empfängt. Unter ‘ihm’ versteht Maurer <hi rend="italic">Kr. Übsch.</hi> II 53 richtig den Dieb, Schmid übersetzt ‘ihnen, den Einfängern’, die dem Richter blosses Strafgeld bei Wiedereinfang selbigen Tages, sonst mehr schulden sollen, nämlich des Diebes Wergeld. Allein dieses trat doch Ine 36 nur ein, wo <hi rend="spaced">Absicht</hi> beim Freilassen (<hi rend="italic">s. d. Gl</hi> 3 a), also Begünstigung vorlag</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">The lord of the court, not the ‘captor’ (as Price thinks), who after all never receives a <hi rend="italic">fine</hi>. By ‘him’ Maurer <hi rend="italic">Kr. Übsch.</hi> II 53 rightly understands the thief; Schmid translates ‘them, the captors’, who are to owe the judge a mere fine if the man is recaptured the same day, otherwise more, namely the thief’s wergild. But according to Ine 36 this, after all, applied only where there was <hi rend="italic">intent</hi> in letting him go (<hi rend="italic">see</hi> that word in <hi rend="italic">Gl</hi> 3 a), i.e. connivance</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="Ine_72.lnote.de.5" n="5" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">81</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">1</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">60 Schilling, nicht Vermögenseinziehung oder Lebenslösung durch Wergeld</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">60 shillings, not confiscation of property or redemption of life by wergild</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="Ine_72.lnote.de.6" n="6" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">81</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">1</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">‘wenn’ Schmid, <hi rend="italic">dummodo</hi> meinend; dieser Sinn kommt für <hi rend="italic">þeah</hi> nicht vor</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">‘if’ Schmid, meaning <hi rend="italic">dummodo</hi>; this sense does not occur for <hi rend="italic">þeah</hi></seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="Ine_72.lnote.de.7" n="7" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">81</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">1</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">Selbigen Tages, im Ggs. zu 73, das besser nicht als neues Kapitel erschiene</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">The same day, in contrast to 73, which had better not appear as a new chapter</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="footnote" xml:id="Ine_72.attnote.fn.15" n="15" source="#attenborough"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">58</biblScope></bibl><label>ðonne</label><seg type="original" xml:lang="en"><hi rend="italic">ðonon</hi>. B.</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="endnote" xml:id="Ine_72.attnote.n.1" n="1" source="#attenborough"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">193</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="en">According to Liebermann <hi rend="italic">wergildþeof</hi> denotes not only a thief, but any criminal who has forfeited his wergeld. He gives a complete list of over twenty offences by which this penalty was incurred; see <hi rend="italic">Glossar</hi>, <hi rend="italic">s.v.</hi> Wergeld, 30.</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="endnote" xml:id="Ine_72.attnote.n.2" n="2" source="#attenborough"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">193</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="en">60 shillings, according to Liebermann (cf. cap. 43).</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="endnote" xml:id="Ine_72.attnote.n.3" n="3" source="#attenborough"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">193</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="en">Liebermann takes <hi rend="italic">him</hi> to be singular—referring to the thief; but cf. cap. 28 § 1 and cap. 36.</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="translationFootnote" xml:id="Ine_72.whinote.n.5" n="5" source="#whitelock"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">407</biblScope></bibl><label>wergild</label><seg type="original" xml:lang="en">literally, “a wergild-thief ”. Liebermann takes it to mean any criminal who would have to pay his wergild to redeem himself.</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="translationFootnote" xml:id="Ine_72.whinote.n.1" n="1" source="#whitelock"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">407</biblScope></bibl><label>them</label><seg type="original" xml:lang="en">Those who let him escape. “Full fine” is 120 shillings. Since <hi rend="italic">þeah</hi> could mean “even if” instead of “yet”, and <hi rend="italic">him</hi> could be “him” as well as “them”, this passage could read: “even if he is recaptured the same night, one has no right to more than full fine from him”, i.e. the thief can no longer be regarded as one caught in the act, and is liable only to the fine of 120 shillings. But the first interpretation fits better with the following clause.</seg></note>
      </div>
    </body>
  </text>
</TEI>
